      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "You are a judge of syntactic circuits. Work only in the current folder.\n1. Read RULES.md completely, including every settled case at its end.\n2. Read the whole file meetings-07-simple.p01.csv: columns n, text, sop, label_state. Each row is a message and one circuit (sop) written for it by a formalizer that saw only the words of that message. Two independent formalizers wrote this same circuit.\n3. Judge every row by RULES.md and write meetings-07-simple.p01.verdicts.jsonl in this folder, one JSON line per row of the CSV: {\"n\": <number>, \"grade\": \"L8\" or \"L1\"..\"L6\", \"note\": \"<one sentence>\"}. L8 accepts. For a rejection the note must quote the words of the message in single quotes and name the wrong statement or the lost information; if you cannot quote one, the circuit is acceptable.\nWork in batch: read the whole CSV, then write the whole verdicts file at once (you may write it in a few large parts); never edit line by line. Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information. Do not edit or create any other file."
      }
    ],
