    "text": "The CSV is multiline-quoted, so 779 lines. I need to parse it properly. Let me write a script to parse the CSV into rows and output n, text, sop. Actually I already read the whole content. But I should parse carefully to be sure the text and sop are exact. Let me use bash with node to parse and dump a compact listing.\n\nLet me use node to parse the CSV properly and print each row's n and text, plus the sop in a readable way. Actually reading the file content above: it's already a full dump with the sop strings. The `\"` quoting inside sop is doubled (`\"\"schedule\"\"`). So I've read everything.\n\nLet me count rows and get list of n values. Let me run a python/node parse.\n\nNow let me judge each row. This is the bulk of the work. Let me be careful.\n\nRules recap for judging: L1 wrong clause/form/verb/aux/negation/elliptical; L2 content word in discourse; L3 wrong wire/ties/nominal head/subject/complement/relation ends/marker/coordination conjunct; L4 states what message doesn't say (ellipsis on fragment sharing no verb, subject not shown, relation between unrelated wires); L5 a clause not written (verb held as modifier/dependent/head/discourse); L6 something untied.\n\nLet me enumerate rows:\n\n1. \"schedule a meeting with Ana tomorrow\" — c1 imperative verb=schedule, modifier=tomorrow, complement=$n1; n1 \"a meeting\" (a dep, meeting head); r1 with from n1 to n2, n2 \"Ana\". All words: schedule, a, meeting, with, Ana, tomorrow. Good. Tomorrow as modifier of clause — fine. L8.\n\n2. \"push the budget sync back by an hour\" — c1 imperative verb=push modifier=back complement=n1; n1 \"the budget sync\"; r1 by from c1 to n2 \"an hour\". Fine. L8.\n\n3. \"when is my dentist appointment?\" — c1 interrogative modifier=when verb=is subject=n1. Question word \"when\" as modifier — settled case says a question word: dependent in \"what colour\", head of nominal in \"what is this?\", modifier in \"where is it?\". So \"when\" as modifier passes. verb=is, subject=my dentist appointment. L8.\n\n4. \"what's on my calendar tomorrow?\" — n1 nominal head=what; c1 interrogative verb=\"'s\" modifier=tomorrow subject=n1; r1 on from c1 to n2 \"my calendar\". Hmm: \"what's\" = \"what\" + \"is\", but here verb is \"'s\" which is fine (form of be as only verb is verb). head \"what\" for n1 as subject — settled: \"the head of a nominal in 'what is this?'\". Good. L8.\n\n5. \"do I have anything with Lee this week?\" — c1 interrogative aux=do verb=have subject=n1 \"I\" complement=n2 \"anything\" complement=n4 \"this week\"; r1 with from n2 to n3 \"Lee\". \"this week\" as complement of have — a nominal the verb takes; acceptable (complement holds nominals whatever they are). Hmm but \"this week\" is a time adverbial; the schema has no adverbial wire except modifier; holding it as complement is allowed since \"complement holds the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\". Is that a wrong statement? Not judged what complement is to verb. So acceptable. L8.\n\n6. \"I'd like a separate calendar for Side project\" — n1 I subject; c1 declarative aux='d verb=like complement=n2; r1 for from n2 to n3 \"Side project\". Fine. L8.\n\n7. \"can you set up a calendar named Kids school?\" — c1 interrogative aux=can verb=set mod=up subject=you complement=n2 \"a calendar\"; c2 nonfinite verb=named complement=n3 \"Kids school\"; r1 from n2 to c2. Fine (participle naming → nonfinite clause tied). L8.\n\n10. \"new calendar: Freelance\" — n1 \"new calendar\", n2 \"Freelance\", c1 fragment complement=n1, r1 from n1 to n2. Fine. L8.\n\n11. \"add one more calendar for me\" — c1 imperative add complement=n1; n1 \"one more calendar\"; r1 attachment=ambiguous marker=for from c1 to n2 \"me\". Fine. L8.\n\n12. \"I need a calendar for running\" — n1 I; c1 declarative need subject=n1 complement=n2 \"a calendar\"; r1 for from n2 to c2 nonfinite verb=running. \"for running\" — running as gerund; nonfinite clause with verb running. Fine. L8.\n\n13. \"add a teal calendar called Book club\" — c1 add complement=n1 \"a teal calendar\"; c2 nonfinite called complement=n2 \"Book club\"; r1 from n1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n14. \"create a calendar named Holidays and make it orange\" — c1 create complement n1; c2 nonfinite named complement n2 Holidays; k1 coordination and conjunct c1 c3; c3 imperative make modifier=orange complement=n3 \"it\"; r1 from n1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n15. \"please make a purple calendar for Choir\" — fine. L8.\n\n16. \"set up a Birthdays calendar in pink\" — c1 set mod up complement n1; r1 in from n1 to n2 \"pink\". Fine. L8.\n\n17. \"could you add a calendar called Garden with a lime green colour?\" — c1 interrogative could add subject you complement n2; c2 nonfinite called complement n3 Garden; r1 with from n3 to n4 \"a lime green colour\"; r2 from n2 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n19. \"make a calendar for Doctor visits and give it a red colour\" — fine. L8.\n\n21. \"would it be possible to get a calendar for the house move? Call it Moving and colour it brown, please\" — c1 interrogative aux=would verb=be modifier=possible subject=it; r1 to from c1 to c2 nonfinite get complement n2; r2 for from n2 to n3 \"the house move\"; c3 imperative Call complement n4 it n5 Moving; k1 and conjunct c3 c4; c4 imperative colour modifier=brown discourse=please complement=n6 it.\n\nConcern: c4 has discourse=\"please\" — but \"please\" is at the end; that's fine (which clause holds a discourse word is not judged). Fine. L8.\n\n23. \"Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar; please add it and make it gold\" — n1 Mihai; c1 declarative verb=asked subject n1; r1 for from c1 to n2 \"a shared Team lunches calendar\"; c2 imperative please add complement n3 it; k1 coordination and conjunct c1, c2, c3; c3 imperative make mod gold complement n4 it.\n\nProblem: k1 coordination joins c1, c2, c3 — \"and\" joins c2 and c3 (the \"add it and make it gold\"). Including c1 in the conjuncts... The semicolon separates sentences. Hmm. Rules: a coordination's conjuncts must be right. Joining \"Mihai asked\" with imperatives in a coordination whose conjunction is \"and\" — the message's \"and\" is between \"add it\" and \"make it gold\". Making c1 a conjunct means the coordination relates c1 which the message does not relate by \"and\". That's L3 (a coordination has a wrong conjunct) or L4 (coordination between wires the message does not relate). Rules L4 mentions \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate.\" Hmm. Which grade? The settled case about \"And\", \"Then\", \"So\" at start of a later sentence: may be discourse or conjunction of coordination of clauses of the two sentences. But here the conjunction is mid-sentence between add and make; c1 is a previous sentence separated by semicolon. The coordination says \"Mihai asked ... and please add it and make it gold\" — three conjuncts joined by one \"and\". This misrepresents: \"and\" doesn't join asked with add. I'd say L3: a coordination has a wrong conjunct. Let me consider: is this within the leniency \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\"? Grammar doesn't allow \"and\" to join across a semicolon with another conjunction present — actually the coordination joins three conjuncts with a single \"and\": that means c1 and c2 and c3 all coordinated by \"and\". The \"and\" is only between c2 and c3. Wait, could it be read as: \"please add it and make it gold\" coordinated, plus... no, k1 includes c1. Hmm, but the semicolon-separated sentence \"Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar\" — with \"and\" not present there. So including c1 is a wrong conjunct. I'll grade L3 and quote 'please add it and make it gold' — quote words of message: \"Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar\" is joined by 'and' to the commands. Quote: 'and'. The note must quote the words of the message in single quotes and name the wrong statement. E.g., \"'Mihai asked ... calendar; please add it and make it gold': 'and' joins 'add' and 'make', but the coordination also holds 'asked', a clause of the earlier sentence\". Grade L3.\n\nHmm, but is it too harsh? The leniency allows differing readings where grammar allows several. The settled case list doesn't allow joining a decl clause from an earlier sentence. Notably similar settled case: \"I walked the team through Sales …; … so as a first pass make ..., then we revisit next week, and give it the title …\" — \"and\" cannot join \"give\" with \"I walked\" across \"make\" (a clause between two conjuncts belonging to none). Here c1 doesn't sit between conjuncts; it's just an extra conjunct at the front. Still a wrong conjunct (the \"and\" doesn't join it). I judge L3.\n\nHmm, wait. But there's an alternative: the coordination conjunct order c1, c2, c3 might be read as coordinating c2 and c3 as well as listing c1? No, k1 is one coordination with conjunction \"and\" and three conjuncts. A coordination \"A and B and C\". The message has \"asked ...; please add it and make it gold\" — hmm actually is it possible the circuit writer intended: c1 conjunct (asked...), and... no.\n\nActually let's reconsider: is it L4 (\"a coordination between wires the message does not relate\")? L4 is about stating what the message doesn't say. L3 says \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct.\" Both could apply; rules say give the first of L1..L6 that applies — L3 comes before L4. So L3.\n\n24. \"a lavender calendar for yoga retreats\" — fragment with complement n1, r1 for n1→n2. Fine. L8.\n\n25. \"add a coral calendar\" — fine.\n\n30. \"give me a reddish calendar for Concerts\" — c1 give complement n1 me, n2 a reddish calendar; r1 for from n2 to n3 Concerts. Fine. L8.\n\n31. \"rename the Gym calendar to Fitness\" — c1 rename complement n1; r1 to from c1 to n2. Fine. L8.\n\n32. \"change the name of Work to Office\" — c1 change complement n1 \"the name\"; r1 of from n1 to n2 Work; r2 to from c1 to n3 Office. Fine. L8.\n\n34. \"can you rename Family to Family and friends?\" — c1 interrogative can rename subject you complement n2 \"Family\"; r1 to from c1 to k1; n3 Family, k1 coordination and conjunct n3 n4 friends. Fine. L8.\n\n35. \"please retitle Gym as Training\" — fine. L8.\n\n36. \"switch the Work calendar's name to Acme\" — c1 switch complement n2 \"name\"; n1 \"the Work calendar\"; r1 's from n2 to n1; r2 to from c1 to n3. Fine — \"'s\" marker from name to the Work calendar. Good. L8.\n\n37. \"I changed jobs last week, so could you rename Work to Northwind?\" — n1 I; c1 declarative changed subject n1 complement n2 jobs complement n3 \"last week\"; k1 so conjunct c1 c2; c2 interrogative could rename subject n4 you complement n5 Work; r1 to from c2 to n6 Northwind. Fine. L8. (\"last week\" as complement — a nominal the verb takes; acceptable.)\n\nHmm, potential issue: L3? \"last week\" as a complement rather than modifier. Not judged. Accept.\n\n41. \"before I share it with Ana, please give the Family calendar a friendlier name, Our home\" — r1 before from c2 to c1 (relation starts at the clause after the comma — settled case says a clause with marker standing between two clauses before a comma opens the clause after the comma; here \"before\" clause precedes comma, so relation from c2 to c1 correct). n1 I; c1 declarative share subject n1 complement n2 it; r2 with from c1 to n3 Ana; c2 imperative please give complement n4 the Family calendar complement n5 a friendlier name; n6 \"Our home\"; r3 from n5 to n6. Fine (name after a name, relation without marker). L8.\n\n42. \"rename this calendar to Personal\" — fine.\n\n43. \"call it Errands\" — fine.\n\n44. \"give the gym calendar a better name\" — fine.\n\n45. \"Work should be Studio now\" — c1 declarative aux should verb be modifier now subject n1 Work complement n2 Studio. Fine. L8.\n\n46. \"new name for Family: Clan\" — n1 new name, r1 for n1→n2 Family, n3 Clan, c1 fragment complement n1, r2 from n1 to n3. Fine. L8.\n\n48. \"make the Gym calendar green\" — fine (modifier per settled case).\n\n49. \"change the colour of Work to dark blue\" — fine.\n\n50. \"can you colour the Family calendar orange?\" — fine (orange modifier).\n\n52. \"switch the Family calendar to a pink color\" — fine.\n\n53. \"recolour the Work calendar teal\" — fine.\n\n54. \"the Family calendar should be yellow\" — c1 declarative aux should verb be modifier yellow subject n1. Fine.\n\n55. \"I can barely see the Gym entries. Change that calendar to a bright orange, please\" — c1 declarative aux can modifier barely verb see subject n1 complement n2; c2 imperative Change discourse please complement n3; r1 to from c2 to n4 \"a bright orange\". Fine. L8.\n\n56. \"if it's not too much trouble, I'd prefer Work in grey; it's less distracting\" — r1 if from c2 to c1; n1 it; c1 declarative verb='s negation=not subject n1 complement n2 \"too much trouble\"; n3 I; c2 declarative aux='d verb prefer subject n3 complement n4 Work; r2 in from n4 to n5 grey; n6 it; c3 declarative verb='s modifier less modifier distracting subject n6; k1 coordination conjunct c2 c3.\n\nCheck: \"less distracting\" as two modifiers — fine. Fine. L8.\n\n61. \"different colour for Work please\" — n1 different colour, r1 for n1→n2 Work, c1 fragment discourse please complement n1. Fine. L8.\n\n64. \"turn Work to silver\" — fine.\n\n65. \"remove Family from my calendars\" — fine.\n\n67. \"can you delete Book club?\" — fine.\n\n69. \"drop the Holidays calendar\" — fine.\n\n70. \"take the Side project calendar out\" — c1 take modifier out complement n1. Fine.\n\n71. \"erase my Family calendar\" — fine.\n\n73. \"the course finished in June. Could you remove the Evening classes calendar now?\" — n1 the course; c1 declarative finished subject n1; r1 in from c1 to n2 June; c2 interrogative Could remove modifier now subject n3 you complement n4. Fine. L8.\n\n74. \"we merged everything into Family, so please delete the old Kids calendar\" — fine.\n\n76. \"if the Moving calendar is empty now, just remove it, thanks\" — r1 if from c2 to c1; c1 declarative is modifier empty modifier now subject n1; c2 imperative modifier just verb remove discourse thanks complement n2 it. Fine — \"thanks\" in discourse; is \"thanks\" content? No, it's a discourse word (listener). L8.\n\n79. \"bin the Choir calendar\" — fine.\n\n80. \"remove that calendar please\" — fine.\n\n82. \"scrap Freelance\" — fine.\n\n84. \"add Tom to my contacts\" — fine.\n\n85. \"create a person called Elena Popescu\" — fine.\n\n89. \"add someone new: Marta Silva\" — c1 imperative add modifier=new complement n1 \"someone\"; n2 \"Marta Silva\"; r1 from n1 to n2. \"new\" as modifier of clause — \"add someone new\": new says what someone is... Settled case about \"a word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause\" — \"new\" here describes \"someone\" as an indefinite with post-modifier. Holding \"new\" as clause modifier is allowed under that settled case (a word after a complement nominal → modifier). Accept. L8.\n\n92. \"I keep typing Carla's name and it doesn't find her. Could you add Carla Mendes as a contact?\" — n1 I; c1 declarative keep subject n1; c2 nonfinite typing complement n3 \"name\"; n2 Carla; r1 's from n3 to n2; k1 and conjunct c1 c3; n4 it; c3 declarative aux does negation n't verb find subject n4 complement n5 her; c4 interrogative Could add subject n6 you complement n7 Carla Mendes; r2 as from c4 to n8 a contact; r3 from c1 to c2.\n\nCheck: c1 \"I keep\" — keep is a verb, complement is the nonfinite clause \"typing...\" tied by r3. Fine. L8.\n\n95. \"our new intern is called Felix Wong; would you create him as a person?\" — n1 our new intern; c1 declarative aux=is verb=called subject n1 complement n2 \"Felix Wong\". Hmm: \"is called\" — auxiliary \"is\" + verb \"called\". That's fine. n2 Felix Wong. c2 interrogative would create subject n3 you complement n4 him; r1 as from c2 to n5 a person; k1 coordination conjunct c1 c2.\n\nConcern: k1 joins c1 and c2 with no conjunction listed (no \"conjunction\" key). The two sentences separated by semicolon. A coordination without conjunction between a declarative and an interrogative from separate sentences — does the message relate them? There's no \"and\"/\"so\" word. So the coordination states a tie the message doesn't state → L4 (a coordination between wires the message does not relate). Hmm, is that harsh? Rules: L4 \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate.\" There's no conjunction word at all. But wait — punctuation stands in no wire, and a coordination without conjunction is possible (asyndetic). Could the semicolon justify joining? The rules say \"Separate sentences need no tie\" (L6 note) implying they don't require one, but adding one... Hmm, is a coordination joining two separate sentences wrong? The settled cases only discuss \"And/Then/So\" as discourse or conjunction. Here there's no such word. A coordination with conjunction missing entirely joining two sentences — I think this is a spurious tie: L4.\n\nHmm, but careful: does k1 hurt downstream? The next layer would see \"our new intern is called Felix Wong\" coordinated with the request. Is that a wrong statement to undo? It states that the two clauses are conjoined. The message separated them with a semicolon but they are related in discourse (\"our new intern is called X; would you create him as a person?\" — the second clearly continues the first). Actually they ARE related — the semicolon and pronoun \"him\" relate them. Is a coordination an acceptable encoding of that relation? Rules L4: coordination between wires that the message does not relate. The message does relate them loosely. Hmm.\n\nConsider similar case in row 105: \"Mihai goes by Mike now, please update his name\" — k1 coordination conjunct c1 c2 with no conjunction word (comma). And row 56: \"it's less distracting\" joined by k1 with no conjunction (semicolon) — c2 and c3. And in row 21 maybe. So this pattern (coordination without conjunction joining two clauses of one message) recurs. The formalizer seems to do this systematically: k1 with no conjunction word joining the statement clause and the request clause.\n\nIs this acceptable? Rules say \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\". Coordination keys are `conjunct`, `conjunction`. Conjunction is optional (no rule requires it). For asyndetic coordination (comma, semicolon), it's legit grammar. A semicolon or comma joining two clauses is grammatically a coordination without conjunction. I think this passes: the words (punctuation) put the clauses side by side. Punctuation stands in no wire, but punctuated juxtaposition is real coordination. I'll accept these as L8, as there's no word quoted that contradicts.\n\nHmm, but \"the message does not relate\" — the messages do relate them (juxtaposed in one message). I'll accept. That's the fair reading: judging fairly, plausible reading.\n\nBack to 95: also check \"is called Felix Wong\": \"called\" as verb with aux \"is\" — fine. L8.\n\n96. \"new person: Dana\" — fine.\n\n97. \"add Leo\" — fine.\n\n102. \"put Zoe in people\" — fine.\n\n103. \"rename Lee to Lee Chen\" — fine.\n\n104. \"change Priya to Priya Nair\" — fine.\n\n105. \"Mihai goes by Mike now, please update his name\" — accepted (see above). L8.\n\n106. \"can you change the name of Ana to Ana Ionescu?\" — fine.\n\n110. \"please call Ana Annie in the people list\" — c1 imperative please call complement n1 \"Ana Annie\"; r1 in from n1 to n2 the people list. Hmm: n1 has head \"Annie\" with dependent \"Ana\" — is that right? \"call Ana Annie\" — Ana is the object, Annie is the new name (object complement). Merging into one nominal \"Ana Annie\" with head Annie: words of one nominal are... Hmm. Two things: a person and a name. Merging two nominals into one is L3 (\"two nominals are merged\"). Is that a wrong statement? A name right after a noun (\"x 120\", \"the node Intro\") may be a dependent in that noun's nominal. Settled case allows a name right after a noun as dependent. Here \"Ana Annie\" — \"Annie\" after \"Ana\" — but which is head? The settled case says a number or a name right after a noun may be a dependent in that noun's nominal. So \"Annie\" as dependent of \"Ana\" would pass; here it's the reverse: \"Ana\" dependent of \"Annie\". Do the words settle that \"Ana\" is the thing and \"Annie\" the name? In \"call X Y\", X is the object. Hmm, but with \"call\" being the naming verb, the reading \"name Annie Ana\"? The message says 'call Ana Annie' — actually one could argue the head should be \"Ana\". Is the head wrong → L3? Hmm. Judging fairly: is it a wrong statement or a plausible reading? \"call Ana Annie\" — \"Ana Annie\" as one nominal with head Annie treats it as a name-phrase. Rules permit a name of several words may stand whole in head... hmm not exactly.\n\nConsider \"rename Lee to Lee Chen\": there the second is n2 with head Chen, dep Lee. And \"change Priya to Priya Nair\" similar. For 110, the object is \"Ana\" and the new name \"Annie\". The formalizer made one nominal \"Ana Annie\" (head Annie), i.e., merged object and complement. Two nominals merged = L3. The words 'call Ana Annie' — the merging loses that \"Ana\" is the thing called. But would the next layer be misled? It would read: call (something named \"Ana Annie\"). Against \"Anna\" existing? Hmm. The instruction: reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information. Merging \"Ana\" and \"Annie\" into one nominal is a wrong statement about which words form each nominal. Is it clearly wrong? Under \"call\", \"Ana Annie\" is not a name of one thing — \"Ana\" is the referent and \"Annie\" the new name. Compare the settled case: \"A number or a name right after a noun ('block 2', 'x 120', 'the node Intro') may be a dependent in that noun's nominal\". That's for appositive naming. Here \"call Ana Annie\" — arguably \"Ana\" and \"Annie\" are two nominals (object + object complement), like \"call it the Hive\" (row 189 has n1 it and n2 the Hive as two complements). Indeed row 189 \"call it the Hive\" splits into two nominals. So the same construction here is merged. Inconsistent. I think L3: the words of two nominals are merged — \"Ana\" is the thing acted on and \"Annie\" the name given.\n\nHmm, but wait: is the head wrong reading maybe passing? Rule L3: \"The words of one nominal are split between wires, or two nominals are merged.\" This is two nominals merged. So L3. Note quoting 'call Ana Annie'.\n\nHold on. Let me consider leniency again: \"a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable\". Merging object and new name into one nominal \"Ana Annie\" — the next layer can still see both words and could split. But rules explicitly list merged nominals as L3 rejection, and I can quote words. The judgment: is it a wrong statement? I'd say yes — it says \"Ana Annie\" is one thing (a name) rather than a call from Ana to Annie. I'll grade L3.\n\nHmm, but let me double check that the settled case doesn't cover it: \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker ('with id neck', 'type radar'): either may be the head.\" That's after a marker. Here there's no marker before \"Ana Annie\". So merge not covered → L3.\n\nActually wait — is it possible to read \"call Ana Annie\" as \"call [someone] Ana Annie\"? Unlikely.\n\nI'll grade 110 L3.\n\n112. \"there are two Lees in the office. Could you rename ours to Lee from Sales?\" — n1 there; c1 declarative are subject n1 complement n2 \"two Lees\"; r1 in from c1 to n3 the office; c2 interrogative Could rename subject n4 you complement n5 ours; r2 to from c2 to n6 Lee; r3 from from n6 to n7 Sales. Fine. L8.\n\n118. \"rename this person to Pri\" — fine.\n\n119. \"Ana becomes Anastasia\" — fine.\n\n120. \"call him Lenny instead\" — c1 imperative call modifier=instead complement n1 him n2 Lenny. Fine. L8.\n\n121. \"Lee should read Lee K.\" — c1 declarative aux should verb read subject n1 Lee complement n2 \"Lee K\" (dep Lee, head K). Fine.\n\n122. \"change her name to Ana Maria\" — fine.\n\n124. \"remove Lee from my people\" — fine.\n\n125. \"please take Mihai off the people list\" — fine.\n\n126. \"can you delete Ana from the contacts?\" — fine.\n\n129. \"erase Priya from people\" — fine.\n\n133. \"if Mihai isn't on any meetings anymore, you can remove him from people\" — r1 if from c2 to c1; n1 Mihai; c1 declarative verb=is negation=n't modifier=anymore subject n1; r2 on from c1 to n2 \"any meetings\"; n3 you; c2 declarative aux=can verb=remove subject n3 complement n4 him; r3 from from c2 to n5 people.\n\nCheck \"any meetings\": \"any\" as dependent, head \"meetings\" — settled: quantity phrase before noun may be dependent. Fine. \"anymore\" as modifier — fine. L8.\n\n136. \"remove her from contacts\" — fine.\n\n137. \"bin Priya\" — fine.\n\n138. \"Mihai can go from the people list\" — fine.\n\n139. \"lose that contact\" — fine.\n\n141. \"create a new meeting room named Studio B\" — fine.\n\n142. \"can you set up a room, the Library?\" — c1 interrogative can set mod up subject you complement n2 \"a room\"; n3 \"the Library\"; r1 from n2 to n3. Fine.\n\n145. \"new room: Maple\" — fine.\n\n147. \"I need the Phone booth as a room\" — fine.\n\n148. \"add a room called Willow with 12 seats\" — c1 add complement n1; c2 nonfinite called complement n2 Willow; r1 with from n1 to n3 \"12 seats\"; r2 from n1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n152. \"add a big room\" — fine.\n\n154. \"add a room called Oak room on the 3rd floor\" — c1 add complement n1; c2 nonfinite called complement n2 \"Oak room\"; r1 on from c1 to n3 \"the 3rd floor\"; r2 from n1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n158. \"can you add a meeting room called Kepler next to the kitchen?\" — c1 interrogative can add subject you complement n2; c2 nonfinite called complement n3 Kepler; r1 attachment=ambiguous marker=\"next to\" from c1 to n4 the kitchen; r2 from n2 to c2. Fine.\n\n164. \"add a room named Vault in the basement and give it 6 seats\" — c1 add complement n1; c2 nonfinite named complement n2 Vault; r1 in from c1 to n3 basement (attachment ambiguous); k1 and conjunct c1 c3; c3 imperative give complement n4 it complement n5 \"6 seats\"; r2 from n1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n165. \"there's a new focus room on the mezzanine. Could you add it as Perch?\" — fine. L8.\n\n166. \"if possible, add the Rooftop room - it's on the top floor, above the canteen\" — r1 if from c2 to c1; c1 fragment modifier=possible; c2 imperative add complement n1; n2 it; c3 declarative verb='s subject n2; r2 on from c3 to n3 top floor; r3 above from c3 to n4 the canteen; r4 from c2 to c3.\n\nHmm, c1 is a fragment \"possible\" — that's fine, a fragment with no verb (\"if possible\"). Is \"possible\" as modifier OK? Yes, fragment with modifier word.\n\nWait, is c1 given `form=fragment` with modifier possible and no complement — fine.\n\nL8.\n\n169. \"new room in the west wing\" — fine.\n\n172. \"Cabin room, in the annex\" — fine.\n\n173. \"add a room somewhere quiet\" — c1 add modifier=somewhere modifier=quiet complement n1 \"a room\". Fine (settled case: word after complement nominal that says how the thing should be is modifier of clause; also \"somewhere\" adverb). L8.\n\n175. \"rename the Cedar room to Redwood\" — fine.\n\n178. \"the Focus pod should be called Pod 1\" — c1 declarative aux=should aux=be verb=called subject n1 complement n2 \"Pod\"; n3 \"1\"; r1 from n2 to n3. Hmm, \"Pod 1\" split into two nominals tied by relation without marker — allowed (number right after noun may be a nominal of its own tied to noun). Fine. L8.\n\n181. \"relabel the cedar room as Room 301\" — fine.\n\n183. \"people keep confusing it with the board room. Could you rename the Cedar room to Cedar studio?\" — c1 declarative keep subject n1 people; c2 nonfinite confusing complement n2 it; r1 with from c2 to n3 board room; c3 interrogative Could rename subject n4 you complement n5 Cedar room; r2 to from c3 to n6 \"Cedar studio\"; r3 from c1 to c2. Fine. L8.\n\n184. \"rename the Focus pod to Phone booth and set its seats to 1\" — c1 rename complement n1; r1 to c1→n2; k1 and conjunct c1 c2; c2 imperative set complement n3 its seats; r2 to from c2 to n4 \"1\". Fine. L8.\n\n188. \"rename this room Atlas\" — fine.\n\n189. \"call it the Hive\" — fine.\n\n190. \"focus pod becomes Pod A\" — fine.\n\n191. \"new name for the cedar room: Spruce\" — fine.\n\n192. \"give the board room a shorter name\" — fine.\n\n194. \"set the Board room to 14 seats\" — fine.\n\n195. \"the Cedar room now fits 10 people\" — c1 declarative modifier=now verb=fits subject n1 complement n2 \"10 people\". Fine.\n\n196. \"change the capacity of the Focus pod to 2\" — fine.\n\n198. \"please make the Cedar room a six-seater\" — fine.\n\n200. \"reduce the cedar room to 8 seats\" — fine.\n\n204. \"set the board room to 18 seats and book it for the weekly review\" — fine.\n\n207. \"Board room: 22 seats\" — n1 Board room, n2 22 seats, c1 fragment complement n1, r1 from n1 to n2. Fine.\n\n208. \"two more seats in the cedar room\" — n1 two more seats; r1 in from n1 to n2; c1 fragment complement n1. Fine.\n\n211. \"fewer seats in the Board room\" — fine.\n\n212. \"Cedar room fits 7\" — c1 declarative fits subject n1 complement n2 \"7\". Fine.\n\n213. \"change that room to 11 seats\" — fine.\n\n214. \"move the Cedar room to the 3rd floor\" — fine.\n\n216. \"change the Focus pod location to the ground floor\" — n1 \"the Focus pod location\"; r1 to from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\n217. \"can you set the location of the board room to the east wing?\" — fine.\n\n219. \"relocate the Board room to the Bucharest office\" — fine.\n\n220. \"set the Cedar room as being on the 1st floor\" — c1 set complement n1; r1 as from c1 to c2 nonfinite verb=being; r2 on from c2 to n2 the 1st floor. Fine.\n\n222. \"the Board room moved during the renovation. Could you change its location to the north wing, by the lifts?\" — fine. L8.\n\n228. \"Board room is in the annex now\" — c1 declarative is modifier now subject n1; r1 in from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\n229. \"put the focus pod on level 3\" — c1 put complement n1; r1 on from c1 to n2 \"level\"; n3 \"3\"; r2 from n2 to n3. Fine.\n\n230. \"move it to the other building\" — fine.\n\n231. \"cedar room location: west wing\" — fine.\n\n234. \"delete the Focus pod\" — fine.\n\n235. \"remove the Cedar room from the rooms list\" — fine.\n\n237. \"can you delete the Oak room?\" — fine.\n\n239. \"take Studio B out of the rooms\" — n1 \"Studio B\" (dep Studio, head B), r1 out of from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\n240. \"drop the cedar room\" — fine.\n\n242. \"move the budget sync to the Focus pod, then remove the Board room\" — fine.\n\n246. \"delete this room\" — fine.\n\n248. \"remove that room please\" — fine.\n\n249. \"bin the focus pod\" — fine.\n\n251. \"scrap the cedar one\" — c1 scrap complement n1 \"the cedar one\"? head \"one\" with deps the, cedar. Fine — \"the cedar one\" is a nominal with head \"one\". L8.\n\n252. \"lose the Oak room\" — fine.\n\n253. \"set up a meeting with Ana and Lee on Thursday at 2pm\" — c1 set mod up complement n1 \"a meeting\"; r1 with from n1 to k1 (Ana and Lee coord); r2 on from n1 to n4 Thursday; r3 at from n1 to n5 2pm.\n\nIssue: relations r2/r3 start at n1 \"a meeting\" — fine (any nominal of that clause grammar allows). But hmm, could \"on Thursday at 2pm\" attach to \"Lee\"? Ambiguity — relation start allowed at nominal of that clause; settled: \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". Starting at n1 is the natural reading. Fine. L8.\n\n254. \"book a call with Priya tomorrow at 10:30\" — c1 book modifier tomorrow complement n1; r1 with from n1 to n2 Priya; r2 at from n1 to n3 10:30. Fine.\n\n255. \"can you schedule a 1:1 with Mihai on Monday at 9am?\" — c1 interrogative can schedule subject you complement n2 \"a 1:1\"; r1 with from n2 to n3 Mihai; r2 on from n2 to n4 Monday; r3 at from n4 to n5 9am. Fine.\n\n258. \"put a Roadmap chat with Ana on my calendar for Friday at 4pm\" — c1 put complement n1; r1 with from n1 to n2 Ana; r2 on from c1 to n3 my calendar; r3 for from c1 to n4 Friday; r4 at from c1 to n5 4pm. Fine.\n\n259. \"invite Lee and Mihai to a meeting on Wednesday at noon\" — c1 invite complement k1; r1 to from c1 to n3 a meeting; r2 on from n3 to n4 Wednesday; r3 at from n3 to n5 noon. Fine.\n\n261. \"Lee wants to go over the launch numbers, so could you set up a meeting with him and Priya on Thursday afternoon?\" — c1 declarative wants subject n1; r1 to from c1 to c2 nonfinite go; r2 over from c2 to n2 launch numbers; k1 so conjunct c1 c3; c3 interrogative could set mod up subject n3 you complement n4 a meeting; r3 with from n4 to k2; k2 him and Priya; r4 on attachment=ambiguous marker=on from c3 to n7 \"Thursday afternoon\". Fine. L8.\n\n264. \"schedule a meeting with Priya on Friday at 2pm and move the budget sync to Monday\" — fine.\n\n267. \"meet Priya on Friday\" — c1 imperative meet complement n1 Priya; r1 on from c1 to n2 Friday. Fine.\n\n269. \"lunch meeting with Lee and Ana\" — n1 lunch meeting; r1 with n1→k1; c1 fragment complement n1. Fine.\n\n270. \"book me in with Priya\" — c1 book modifier in complement n1 me; r1 with from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\n271. \"set up something with Lee\" — fine.\n\n272. \"I need to see Mihai tomorrow\" — n1 I; c1 declarative need subject n1; r1 to from c1 to c2 nonfinite see modifier tomorrow complement n2 Mihai. Fine.\n\nHmm, so I've judged everything L8 except maybe 23 and 110. That seems too lenient? Let me re-check rows more carefully, especially the ones where I might have missed details. Let me verify word coverage/parsing with a script: the RULES say a program already checked every word stands in exactly one value. But I should double check for subtle errors in the sop that I may have skimmed.\n\nLet me re-examine each row's sop against the text thoroughly with a script that prints text and sop side by side per row. Then focused review of specific candidate issues:\n\nRow 5: \"do I have anything with Lee this week?\" — c1 has aux=do, verb=have, subject n1, complement n2 anything, complement n4 \"this week\". Hmm \"this week\" held as complement of have. Alternatively modifier. Not judged (complement vs modifier? \"complement holds the nominals the verb takes, whatever they are to it\"; \"this week\" is a nominal). Fine.\n\nRow 3: fine.\n\nRow 4: \"what's on my calendar tomorrow?\" — verb \"'s\", subject n1 head \"what\", r1 on from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\nRow 21: \"Call it Moving and colour it brown, please\" — c3 Call complement n4 it n5 Moving. Two complements: it and Moving. Fine (like \"call it the Hive\").\n\nRow 41: \"before I share it with Ana, please give the Family calendar a friendlier name, Our home\" — r3 from n5 to n6 markerless: \"Our home\" as a second name after \"name\". Fine.\n\nNow let me double-check some specific rows where the sop might hide something. Let me systematically print each row for careful re-reading. I read the whole dump already, but doing a careful second pass with a script listing n and text helps catch mismatch.\n\nActually let me reconsider row 23 more carefully, and also think about whether other rows have coordination with extra conjuncts.\n\nRow 23: k1 conjunct c1, c2, c3 with conjunction \"and\". c1 = \"Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar\" (sentence 1). c2 = \"please add it\", c3 = \"make it gold\". The \"and\" in the message joins c2 and c3 only. So including c1 is a wrong conjunct → L3. I'm fairly confident this should be rejected. Is there a lenient reading? \"Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar; please add it and make it gold\" — a three-way coordination with a semicolon before? No. The settled case: \"And/Then/So at the start of a later sentence may be a discourse word... or the conjunction of a coordination of the clauses of the two sentences.\" Here \"and\" is not at sentence start. The last settled case in RULES: coordination can't join across an intervening clause belonging to none. Here c1 doesn't intervene between conjuncts. But is c1 a legitimate conjunct? No word joins it. I'll reject with L3.\n\nHmm, but wait. What grade for a coordination with a extra conjunct? L3 explicitly: \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct.\" Yes L3.\n\nNow let me also reconsider row 95's k1 — coordination with no conjunction joining c1 and c2 (separate sentences). And row 105 same. And row 56 same. And row 92's k1 joins c1 and c3 with \"and\" — that's a real \"and\". Fine.\n\nRow 95: is the coordination harmful? The message: \"our new intern is called Felix Wong; would you create him as a person?\" No conjunction. Coordination with no conjunction between two sentences. Rules L4: \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate.\" Do the words relate them? The semicolon juxtaposition + \"him\". I lean accept: the two clauses are juxtaposed in one message and clearly linked by \"him\". A coordination without a conjunction is punctuation-based and punctuation is not judged... Hmm, \"Punctuation stands in no wire\" means punctuation isn't in any wire's values, but it can motivate structure.\n\nConsider consistency: if I reject 23 for having an extra conjunct joined by nothing, should I reject 95 for a coordination joined by nothing? Interesting — but the direction differs. In 23 a word (\"and\") joins two clauses and the circuit wrongly adds a third. That's a wrong statement: it claims the request is coordinated with the statement via \"and\". Hmm, actually in both cases the coordination says \"these clauses stand side by side\", which for 95 is true-ish (they do stand side by side, semicolon-joined), and for 23 also they stand side by side... Ugh.\n\nLet me think about what the next layer does. It reads the circuit and decides the meaning with context. A coordination of c1, c2, c3 with conjunction \"and\": the next layer sees a three-way \"and\". Does it need to undo that? It would see \"Mihai asked for X, and please add it, and make it gold\". The truth: \"add it and make it gold\" — the added conjunct \"asked\" doesn't change what to do: the request is add+make. But it falsely ties the asking to the request — the request is a consequence of the asking, which the next layer can infer anyway.\n\nHmm. And 95's coordination ties a declarative statement to the request, which is precisely the relation the semicolon gives. In 23 the semicolon is also there. Frankly both are \"clauses side by side in one message\". So they should be judged alike. So either both L8 or both L3.\n\nGiven the rule \"Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information\", and that the coordination includes both sentences' clauses which are indeed juxtaposed in one message... I could accept both. But the \"and\" in 23 pairs specifically add+make. Includes c1 additionally.\n\nHmm, let's look at whether rules give an explicit pass for this. \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it.\" This constrains the start of the coordination, and here it starts at c1 — which does not end right before \"and\" nor holds it. c1 ends far before, and doesn't hold c2/c3 in any relation. So per that rule, k1 starting at c1 with \"and\" as conjunction violates \"the earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction\". → the coordination has wrong conjuncts → L3. Good, that's grounded.\n\nAnd for 95 (no conjunction), the rule about \"earlier wire a coordination joins\" concerns conjunctions. Asyndetic coordination... The 95 case joins c1 (declarative, semicolon) with c2. Since the message does juxtapose them, accept.\n\nHmm, but is there something \"the message does not relate\"? The semicolon relates them. Fine, accept 95.\n\nNow check 105: \"Mihai goes by Mike now, please update his name\" — k1 joins c1 and c2 without conjunction; comma juxtaposition. Accept.\n\nRow 56: k1 conjunct c2, c3 with no conjunction, semicolon separates \"I'd prefer Work in grey\" and \"it's less distracting\". Accept.\n\nRow 41: r1 before from c2 to c1 — correct per settled case? The settled case: \"A clause with a marker that stands between two clauses, after a dash, a semicolon or a full stop and before a comma, opens the clause after the comma\". Here \"before\" is at the start of the message, before the first clause, and the relation goes from c2 (the command after the comma) to c1 (\"before I share it with Ana\"). That's the standard reading: relation starts at the clause after the comma (c2) and ends at the \"before\" clause (c1). Correct.\n\nNow let me re-scan for other subtle problems I may have rushed:\n\nRow 2: \"push the budget sync back by an hour\" — n1 \"the budget sync\": deps the, budget; head sync. Fine. r1 by from c1 to n2 \"an hour\". Fine.\n\nRow 7: \"can you set up a calendar named Kids school?\" — c2 nonfinite named complement n3 \"Kids school\". Fine.\n\nRow 12: \"I need a calendar for running\" — c2 nonfinite verb running, no complement. Fine.\n\nRow 14: fine.\n\nRow 17: r1 with from n3 (Garden) to n4 \"a lime green colour\". Hmm — could \"with a lime green colour\" attach to \"calendar\" instead of \"Garden\"? Both names... The relation starts at any nominal of that clause grammar allows. n3 Garden is in a nonfinite clause; settled case about \"of\" restricts. For \"with\", a nominal of the clause... n3 is a nominal of clause c2, not of c1. Rules for relation start: \"the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\" where \"that clause\" is the clause the relation belongs to. Which clause does r1 belong to? It starts at n3, so c2. Grammar allows \"Garden with a lime green colour\" — the name with a colour is odd but the calendar with a lime green colour is natural. Hmm, does the words rule out n3? \"a calendar called Garden with a lime green colour\" — attaches to calendar (which one gets the colour). But since it starts at n3 = Garden, which is the head of the same thing, the next layer can fix. Also the settled case: \"[of] after a nominal: the relation starts at that nominal or at its clause, never at another nominal\" — different marker. For \"with\", grammar allows attaching to Garden's phrase? \"a calendar called Garden with a lime green colour\" — \"Garden with a lime green colour\" reads as a name phrase; plausible. I'll accept L8.\n\nRow 19: \"make a calendar for Doctor visits and give it a red colour\" — n2 \"Doctor visits\": dep Doctor, head visits. Fine.\n\nRow 30: fine.\n\nRow 36: fine.\n\nRow 45: \"Work should be Studio now\" — fine.\n\nRow 46: \"new name for Family: Clan\" — r2 from n1 to n3: \"new name for Family Clan\"? Fine.\n\nRow 49: fine.\n\nRow 54: \"the Family calendar should be yellow\" — yellow as modifier, fine (settled: word after complement that says how the thing should be — here \"be yellow\", modifier fine).\n\nRow 55: fine.\n\nRow 61: fine.\n\nRow 70: \"take the Side project calendar out\" — fine.\n\nRow 76: fine.\n\nRow 85: \"create a person called Elena Popescu\" — n2 dep Elena head Popescu. Fine.\n\nRow 89: discussed. \"add someone new: Marta Silva\" — modifier \"new\" of clause; r1 from n1 someone to n2 Marta Silva. Accept.\n\nRow 92: c1 \"I keep\" subject n1; r3 from c1 to c2 (typing). c2 nonfinite typing complement n3 \"name\". Fine. Also c3 \"it doesn't find her\" — aux does, negation n't, verb find — negation word in clause. Fine.\n\nRow 106: r2 to from c1 to n4 \"Ana Ionescu\". Fine.\n\nRow 110: rejected L3 (merged nominals?). Let me reconsider once more. Hmm, \"call Ana Annie in the people list\". Alternative: maybe \"Ana Annie\" as one nominal is like \"the node Intro\" — a name right after a noun. Settled case explicitly: \"A number or a name right after a noun ('block 2', 'x 120', 'the node Intro') may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied to the noun by a relation without a marker. Both pass.\" Here \"Annie\" is a name right after the noun \"Ana\". So \"Annie\" as dependent in \"Ana\"'s nominal is explicitly allowed! But the circuit has it the other way: \"Ana\" as dependent of \"Annie\" (head Annie). Does the settled case allow either head? It says the name (second) may be a dependent in the noun's nominal — head is the noun (Ana). Here head is Annie, the name, and the noun is the dependent. That's the reverse of the settled allowance. Hmm.\n\nRules: \"Two bare nouns side by side after a marker: either may be the head.\" Not after a marker here.\n\nSo is head \"Annie\" wrong → L3 \"The head is wrong\"? The message \"call Ana Annie\": Ana is the person, Annie is the label. The head of the nominal that refers to a thing should be Ana. But under the call construction, the natural structural reading is two complements: \"call\" + object \"Ana\" + object complement \"Annie\" (like \"call it the Hive\" in row 189, \"call him Lenny\" row 120, \"call it Errands\" row 43 — all split into two nominals). So this row merges them, deviating from the construction and from the pattern. And the head being the new name makes \"Ana\" a mere qualifier. I judge L3: two nominals merged (the person and the new name).\n\nHmm, but is the merge a \"wrong statement\"? It says \"Ana Annie\" is one thing. The next layer can still see both words in order; it might read the head \"Annie\"... Risk of accepting: rule says merged nominals = L3. I'll reject.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the leniency hooks: \"Where several readings pass\" — not applicable. So L3 stands.\n\nBut hold on, I should double check: were there other rows where two nominals get merged, or where the head is unusual but similar? Let me check rows with names: 103 \"rename Lee to Lee Chen\" → n2 dep Lee head Chen. That's after marker \"to\", two bare nouns after a marker → either may be head. Passes.\n\nRow 110 is not after a marker. Hmm.\n\nNow, am I being too harsh on 110 while being lenient elsewhere? The difference: explicit settled allowance covers dependent-in-noun case (head = the noun, i.e., \"Ana\") but not the reverse. So there is a real deviation.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider: is \"Annie\" being head really \"wrong\"? The relation between \"Ana\" and \"Annie\": \"Ana\" dependent of \"Annie\". The settled case allows \"x 120\" (number dependent of noun) and \"the node Intro\" — Intro dependent of node. So the second word is dependent; the first is head. Here inverted. I'll go L3, note: \"'call Ana Annie': 'Ana' and 'Annie' are held as one nominal with 'Annie' as head, merging the thing called with the name given\".\n\nNow, let me do a careful second pass to catch things I might have missed. I'll write a script that parses the CSV and prints rows in a compact form so I can re-read the sops attentively. Rather than trusting my single read, let me verify a few specific things:\n\n- Check every row: does the clause have form per message type?\n- Check negation and auxiliaries.\n- Check subject presence where words show one (e.g., imperative with \"you\"?).\n\nLet me look for rows where the subject is missing though the words show one — e.g., row 112 \"there are two Lees\" subject \"there\" fine.\n\nRow 73: \"the course finished in June.\" fine.\n\nRow 178: \"the Focus pod should be called Pod 1\" — aux should, aux be, verb called. Hmm: two auxiliaries is fine (\"should be called\"). But is \"be\" an auxiliary and \"called\" the verb? Yes. Fine.\n\nRow 220: \"set the Cedar room as being on the 1st floor\" — c1 set complement n1; r1 as from c1 to c2 nonfinite being; r2 on from c2 to n2. Fine.\n\nRow 222: fine.\n\nRow 253: \"a meeting with Ana and Lee\" — r1 with from n1 to k1. Fine.\n\nRow 259: \"invite Lee and Mihai to a meeting on Wednesday at noon\" — c1 invite complement k1 where k1 = Lee and Mihai. Fine.\n\nRow 261: k2 him and Priya. Fine.\n\nNow, are there rows where a clause of the message is missing (L5)? Let me check multi-clause messages: 21 (3 clauses: would it be possible, get, Call, colour = 4 clauses) — c1, c2, c3, c4. Yes all. Row 23 has c1, c2, c3. Row 37 two clauses. Row 41 two. Row 55 two. Row 56 three. Row 73 two. Row 74 two. Row 76 two. Row 92: \"I keep typing Carla's name and it doesn't find her. Could you add...\" → c1, c2 (typing), c3, c4. Yes. Row 95 two. Row 105 two. Row 112 two. Row 165 two. Row 166: \"if possible, add the Rooftop room - it's on the top floor, above the canteen\" → c1 fragment possible, c2 add, c3 's. Yes.\n\nRow 183: \"people keep confusing it with the board room. Could you rename...\" → c1 keep, c2 confusing, c3 rename. Yes.\n\nRow 148: \"add a room called Willow with 12 seats\" — c1, c2. Yes.\n\nRow 154: yes.\n\nRow 164: yes.\n\nRow 184: yes.\n\nRow 204: yes.\n\nRow 242: yes.\n\nRow 264: yes.\n\nRow 272: yes.\n\nOK. Now let me look for possible L6 (untied). E.g., row 148: r1 with from n1 to n3 \"12 seats\" — tied. Fine.\n\nRow 154: n3 \"the 3rd floor\" tied from c1 by r1. Fine. Row 208, 211: fine.\n\nRow 231: \"cedar room location: west wing\" — n1, n2, c1 fragment complement n1, r1 from n1 to n2. Fine.\n\nRow 207: fine.\n\nRow 229: \"put the focus pod on level 3\" — n2 level, n3 3, r2 from n2 to n3. Fine.\n\nRow 212: \"Cedar room fits 7\" — n2 head \"7\" as complement. Fine.\n\nRow 178: n2 Pod, n3 1 tied. Fine.\n\nRow 145, 96, 46, 10: fine.\n\nLet me now check for rows where message has a word not accounted — the rules say a program checked, but let me verify with a script anyway to catch e.g. a word missing/misplaced in ways I overlooked, and to double-check my reading of each row's sop. I'll write a node script parsing the CSV, extracting quoted values per row, and diffing against the text's word list. That's a good sanity check.\n\nLet me do that.",
