    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Good: only clitic splits differ, which is expected and fine. 150 rows, all words accounted.\n\nNow let me print a per-row compact review to double-check clause forms/verbs. Actually I've reviewed them. But let me be extra careful about a few categories I might have rushed:\n\n1. Commands whose first word could be noun or verb held as noun (L1). Check rows like 2 \"push\", 64 \"turn\", 70 \"take\", 79 \"bin\", 82 \"scrap\", 137, 139 \"lose\", 240, 249, 251, 252 — all have verb. Good.\n\n2. Rows where \"form\" should be interrogative with question mark but is declarative. Check: 3 interrogative, 4 interrogative, 5 interrogative, 7 interrogative, 17 interrogative, 21 interrogative, 34 interrogative, 50 interrogative, 67 interrogative, 73 c2 interrogative, 92 c4 interrogative, 95 c2 interrogative, 106, 112 c2, 126, 142, 158, 165 c2, 183 c3, 217, 222 c2, 237, 255, 261 c3. All ✓.\n\n3. Rows with \"please\" - discourse fine.\n\n4. Imperatives containing \"can you\"? no.\n\n5. What about row 21 c1 \"would it be possible\" — interrogative ✓.\n\n6. Row 45 \"Work should be Studio now\" — declarative ✓.\n\n7. Row 119, 190 declarative ✓.\n\n8. Row 195 \"the Cedar room now fits 10 people\" — \"now\" modifier ✓.\n\n9. Row 228 \"Board room is in the annex now\" — modifier now ✓.\n\nNow let me reconsider ambiguous judgments:\n\nRow 166: c1 is a fragment \"possible\" with modifier=possible. \"if possible\" — fragment with modifier. Fine. Also r1 if from c2 to c1 (relation from the command clause to the \"if\" clause). The settled case pattern: \"r1 if from $c2 to $c1\" — the relation starts at the clause after the comma (c2). ✓.\n\nRow 170? Not present.\n\nLet me also check row 110 more: also n1's head \"Annie\" with dependent \"Ana\" — note the naming; I decided L3.\n\nLet me reconsider row 89: \"add someone new: Marta Silva\" — \"new\" as modifier of clause. Settled case: \"A word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause\" — \"new\" says what someone is, not how they should be. Hmm. Alternatively \"new\" could be a dependent of \"someone\" (settled: \"everything else\"/\"else\" is a dependent of the pronoun). \"someone new\" — \"new\" postmodifies \"someone\", so a dependent of \"someone\" would be natural; the formalizer put it on the clause. Is that lost information? The grader in the settled case allows clause modifier for words after a complement nominal. So pass.\n\nRow 173: \"add a room somewhere quiet\" — somewhere and quiet both modifiers of clause. \"quiet\" describes the room; pass by the same settled case.\n\nRow 195: \"10 people\" as complement n2 — fine.\n\nRow 5: \"anything with Lee this week\" — n4 \"this week\" complement. Fine.\n\nRow 148: \"with 12 seats\" relation from n1 (a room) to n3. Fine.\n\nRow 158: marker \"next to\" as one marker — fine.\n\nRow 239: \"out of\" one marker — fine.\n\nRow 222: \"by\" from c2 attachment ambiguous — fine.\n\nRow 66? no.\n\nNow, other possibly wrong: Row 92 r1 marker \"'s\" from n3 (name) to n2 (Carla) — \"'s\" from name to Carla: \"Carla's name\" → relation ends at Carla with marker 's starting at name. Fine ✓ (like row 36).\n\nRow 41: r3 from n5 to n6 without marker — \"a friendlier name, Our home\". Fine.\n\nRow 46: r2 from n1 to n3. Fine.\n\nRow 10: r1 from n1 to n2. Fine.\n\nRow 96: fine.\n\nRow 23: also note c1 \"asked\" has no complement but r1 with \"for\" from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\nHmm, one more consideration on Row 23: What if the intended reading is that k1 with conjunction \"and\" joining c1,c2,c3 is a \"coordination of the clauses of the two sentences\"? The settled case about \"And/Then/So\" concerns a coordinating word at the start of a later sentence. Here \"and\" is between \"add it\" and \"make it gold\" — within the second sentence. So no.\n\nAlso, is c2 \"please add it\" correct as a clause of the \"and\" coordination? Yes, \"and\" joins \"add\" and \"make\"; c2 is add. But k1 lists all three. So the wrong part is including c1.\n\nAlternatively the conjunction and conjuncts could be a \"coordination of the clauses of the two sentences\" if we read the message as one: \"Mihai asked for X; please add it and make it gold\". Nope, \"and\" is not at the start of the second sentence.\n\nSo row 23: L3.\n\nNow let me scan again for any row where the circuit might state an `ellipsis` wrongly (L4) — none have ellipsis at all. Are there fragments that clearly share a verb without ellipsis (L1)? E.g., \"make A blue and B green\" style. Check rows with coordination of nominals: 34 (Family and friends) — nominal coord, fine. 253 (Ana and Lee). 259. 261 (him and Priya). 269. Fine. No verb-sharing fragments.\n\nAny message with two sentences where the second lacks a verb and shares one? Row 166 \"it's on the top floor\" has verb. Row 55 second sentence has verb. Row 73. Row 183. Row 95. Row 112. Row 165. Row 222. All fine.\n\nNow subjects: any message with subject shown but circuit lacks it? Check declaratives: all have subject. Interrogatives with \"you\": present. Row 4: \"what's on my calendar\" subject = n1 \"what\" ✓ (that's the subject). Row 3: \"when is my dentist appointment?\" subject = n1 ✓ (when as modifier).\n\nRow 45: subject Work ✓.\nRow 54 ✓. Row 56 c1 subject it ✓; c3 subject it ✓. Row 92 c1 subject I ✓, c3 subject it ✓. Row 95 c1 subject n1 ✓. Row 105 c1 subject Mihai ✓. Row 112 c1 subject there ✓. Row 119 ✓. Row 121 ✓. Row 138 ✓. Row 133 c1 subject Mihai ✓, c2 subject you ✓. Row 195 ✓. Row 212 ✓. Row 228 ✓. Row 178 ✓. Row 190 ✓.\n\nNonfinite clauses: 7, 12, 13, 14, 17, 21, 85, 92, 141, 148, 154, 158, 164, 183, 220, 261, 272 — verbs present.\n\nRow 220 c2 \"being\" nonfinite with r1 as from c1 to c2 — fine.\n\nNow, any row where a content word is in discourse (L2)? Check discourse values: 15 please; 21 c4 please; 23 c2 please; 35 please; 41 c2 please; 55 c2 please; 61 please; 74 c2 please; 76 c2 thanks; 80 please; 105 c2 please; 110 please; 125 please; 198 please; 248 please. All are genuine listener words. ✓\n\nAny L5 (verb held as modifier/head/dependent)? Check \"named\"/\"called\" as nonfinite verbs ✓. Check row 105 \"goes by Mike\" verb goes ✓. Row 110 \"call\" verb ✓. Row 43/189 call ✓.\n\nRow 45 \"should be Studio\": verb be ✓.\n\nHmm row 178: \"should be called Pod 1\" aux=\"be\" verb=called ✓.\n\nRow 95: aux=\"is\" verb=\"called\" ✓.\n\nRow 12: \"for running\" — verb running nonfinite ✓ (gerund).\n\nNow L6 checks: things untied. Let me check each relation start/end exists and every nominal is tied. Consider row 21: n6 \"it\" is complement of c4 ✓.\n\nRow 166: n2 \"it\" subject of c3 ✓; c3 tied to c2 by r4 ✓.\n\nRow 41: n6 tied by r3 ✓.\n\nRow 46: n3 tied ✓.\n\nRow 10: n2 tied ✓.\n\nRow 24: n2 tied by r1 ✓.\n\nRow 96: ✓.\n\nRow 145: ✓. Row 207: ✓. Row 231: ✓. Row 191: ✓.\n\nRow 148: n3 \"12 seats\" tied ✓, c2 tied by r2 ✓.\n\nRow 154: c2 tied, n3 tied ✓.\n\nRow 164: c2 tied ✓, c3 has complement n4 it, n5 seats ✓.\n\nRow 89: n2 tied by r1 ✓.\n\nRow 110: n1 tied by r1 ✓.\n\nRow 142: n3 tied by r1 ✓.\n\nRow 34: n3 tied inside k1 ✓ and k1 is end of r1 ✓.\n\nRow 259: n1 inside k1 ✓.\n\nRow 261: k2 ✓.\n\nRow 92: all tied ✓ (\"typing\" complement n3, n3 tied by r1).\n\nRow 73: fine.\n\nRow 253: n2/n3 in k1 ✓.\n\nRow 269: fine.\n\nRow 258: n3, n4, n5 tied from c1 ✓.\n\nRow 264: fine.\n\nRow 272: c2 tied by r1 ✓.\n\nRow 56: c3 tied via k1 ✓; n2 \"too much trouble\" is complement of c1 ✓; n4 Work ✓; n5 grey tied ✓; n6 it subject ✓.\n\nRow 76: fine.\n\nRow 133: fine.\n\nRow 222: n6 the lifts tied ✓.\n\nRow 216: fine.\n\nRow 217: fine.\n\nOK. Now let me reconsider some potentially-wrong \"form\" cases: Row 76 c2 \"just remove it, thanks\" imperative ✓. Row 166 c1 fragment ✓.\n\nNow let me reconsider two rows where the relation start might be wrong per the settled \"of\" case:\n\nRow 32: \"change the name of Work to Office\" — r1 of from n1 (name) to n2 (Work) ✓ (\"of after a nominal starts at that nominal\").\nRow 49: r1 of from n1 colour to n2 Work ✓.\nRow 106: r1 of from n2 (name) to n3 Ana ✓.\nRow 196: r1 of from n1 capacity to n2 pod ✓.\nRow 217: r1 of from n2 location to n3 board room ✓.\n\nGood.\n\nRow 41: r2 with from c1 to n3 Ana — \"before I share it with Ana\". ✓\n\nRow 92: r2 as from c4 to n8. ✓\n\nRow 112: r3 from from n6 (Lee) to n7 Sales — \"Lee from Sales\". Fine.\n\nRow 2: r1 by from c1 to n2. ✓\n\nRow 200: \"reduce the cedar room to 8 seats\" r1 to from c1 to n2 \"8 seats\" ✓.\n\nRow 204: r1 to from c1 to n2 \"18 seats\" ✓ (no attachment ambiguity but allowed).\n\nRow 213: \"change that room to 11 seats\" — r1 to from c1 to n2 \"11 seats\". Hmm, \"set the Board room to 14 seats\" (194) used attachment=ambiguous. Difference isn't judged.\n\nRow 251: \"scrap the cedar one\" — head \"one\" ✓.\n\nRow 269: \"lunch meeting with Lee and Ana\" — head \"meeting\", dep \"lunch\" ✓.\n\nRow 173: \"somewhere quiet\" both modifiers ✓.\n\nRow 1: \"tomorrow\" modifier ✓ (time word after complement... fine, it's a modifier of clause).\n\nRow 254: \"tomorrow\" modifier and \"at 10:30\" from n1. Fine.\n\nRow 253: \"on Thursday at 2pm\" both from n1 ✓.\n\nHmm what about row 253: \"at 2pm\" from n1 rather than from n4 Thursday — fine per ambiguity allowance.\n\nSo overall only 23 and 110 rejected? Let me re-scan a few rows I might have glossed: 4, 5, 30, 36, 44, 52, 56, 61, 84, 102, 110, 120, 147, 178, 194, 200, 220, 239.\n\nRow 84: \"add Tom to my contacts\" — r1 attachment ambiguous from c1 to n2. ✓\n\nRow 102: \"put Zoe in people\" ✓.\n\nRow 147: \"I need the Phone booth as a room\" — r1 as from c1 to n3 ✓.\n\nRow 30: \"give me a reddish calendar for Concerts\" — complement n1 me, n2 calendar; r1 for from n2 ✓.\n\nRow 44: ✓.\n\nRow 52: ✓.\n\nRow 61: \"different colour for Work please\" — c1 fragment discourse please complement n1 ✓.\n\nRow 120: \"call him Lenny instead\" — \"instead\" modifier ✓.\n\nRow 200/213: ✓.\n\nNow, one more category: \"a nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\" (L3). Check row 183: c2 nonfinite \"confusing\" complement n2 it ✓, r1 with from c2 to n3 ✓. Fine.\n\nRow 92: c2 \"typing\" complement n3 \"name\" ✓ (typing Carla's name) — the relation 's ties name to Carla ✓.\n\nRow 166: r2 on from c3 to n3 ✓ ; c3 \"it's on the top floor, above the canteen\" ✓.\n\nRow 55: c1 \"I can barely see the Gym entries\" — \"barely\" modifier of clause ✓.\n\nRow 21: c2 nonfinite \"get\" complement n2; r2 for from n2 to n3 ✓.\n\nRow 41: c1 \"I share it\" — complement n2 it ✓.\n\nRow 37: c1 complement n2 jobs, n3 last week ✓.\n\nRow 74: c1 \"we merged everything into Family\" — complement n2 everything, r1 into from c1 to n3 ✓.\n\nRow 76: c1 \"the Moving calendar is empty now\" — modifier empty, now ✓.\n\nRow 95: c1 aux is verb called complement n2 Felix Wong ✓.\n\nRow 105: c1 \"Mihai goes by Mike now\" — modifier now; r1 by ✓.\n\nRow 112: c1 \"there are two Lees in the office\" — complement n2, r1 in from c1 ✓.\n\nRow 133: c1 \"Mihai isn't on any meetings anymore\" — negation n't ✓, r2 on ✓, modifier anymore ✓.\n\nRow 165: c1 \"there's a new focus room on the mezzanine\" ✓.\n\nRow 222: c1 \"the Board room moved during the renovation\" ✓.\n\nRow 228: ✓.\n\nRow 122: \"change her name to Ana Maria\" ✓.\n\nRow 124: ✓.\n\nRow 136: ✓.\n\nRow 129: ✓.\n\nRow 126: ✓.\n\nRow 118: ✓.\n\nRow 103/104: ✓.\n\nRow 121: \"Lee should read Lee K.\" — n2 head \"K\" dep Lee ✓ (name after name inside a \"to be read as\" reading; fine).\n\nRow 119: ✓.\n\nRow 190: ✓.\n\nRow 188: ✓.\n\nRow 98-101: not present.\n\nSo the final judgment: 148 L8, 23 L3, 110 L3.\n\nHmm, that gives only 2 rejections out of 150 (1.3%). Is that plausible? The task says the formalizer is decent and two independent formalizers agreed. The `label_state` = \"agreement\" for all rows. Rows in the \"-simple\" file are where spaCy and hmm-rules agree. So a high acceptance rate is plausible.\n\nBut let me be more skeptical and re-read the sops one more time looking for errors in less-obvious places — particularly:\n- Auxiliary/verb assignment (\"switch\", \"recolour\" fine).\n- Whether a relation's `from` starts in another clause (L3: \"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\").\n\nCheck relations crossing clauses:\nRow 7: r1 from n2 (n2 in c1) to c2 ✓.\nRow 13: same ✓. Row 17: r2 from n2 to c2 ✓; r1 from n3 to n4 — both inside c2 ✓.\nRow 21: r1 to from c1 to c2 ✓; r2 from n2 (c2) to n3 (c2) ✓.\nRow 23: r1 for from c1 to n2 ✓.\nRow 37: r1 to from c2 to n6 ✓.\nRow 41: r1 before from c2 to c1 ✓ (settled).\nRow 56: r1 if from c2 to c1 ✓.\nRow 76: r1 if from c2 to c1 ✓.\nRow 92: r1 's from n3 to n2 ✓; r2 as from c4 to n8 ✓; r3 from c1 to c2 ✓.\nRow 95: r1 as from c2 to n5 ✓.\nRow 133: r1 if from c2 to c1 ✓; r3 from c2 to n5 ✓.\nRow 148: r1 with from n1 to n3 ✓; r2 from n1 to c2 ✓.\nRow 154: r1 on from c1 to n3 ✓ — could \"on the 3rd floor\" attach to Oak room in c2? Grammar allows relation start at c1 or a nominal of c1 (a room). Starting at c1 fine.\nRow 158: r1 next to from c1 to n4 ✓.\nRow 164: r1 in from c1 to n3 ✓.\nRow 165: r1 on from n2 to n3 ✓.\nRow 166: r4 from c2 to c3 ✓ (ties the \"it's on the top floor\" clause).\nRow 183: r3 from c1 to c2 ✓.\nRow 220: r1 as from c1 to c2 ✓; r2 on from c2 to n2 ✓.\nRow 261: r1 to from c1 to c2 ✓; r3 with from n4 to k2 ✓; r4 on from c3 ✓.\nRow 272: r1 to from c1 to c2 ✓.\n\nNow, potential L3: Row 148 r1 \"with\" from n1 \"a room\" to n3 \"12 seats\" — fine.\n\nRow 158: r1 \"next to the kitchen\" from c1 — fine.\n\nAnother thing to check: nominal heads that might be wrong.\nRow 70: \"the Side project calendar\" deps the, Side, project; head calendar ✓.\nRow 73: \"the Evening classes calendar\" head calendar ✓.\nRow 74: \"the old Kids calendar\" ✓.\nRow 44: \"the gym calendar\" ✓.\nRow 36: \"the Work calendar's name\" ✓.\nRow 110: head Annie (discussed).\nRow 207: \"Board room\" dep Board head room ✓.\nRow 212 ✓. Row 251 ✓. Row 269 \"lunch meeting\" ✓.\nRow 216: \"the Focus pod location\" head location ✓.\nRow 217: \"the location of the board room\" ✓.\nRow 231: \"cedar room location\" dep cedar, room; head location ✓.\nRow 141: \"a new meeting room\" ✓.\nRow 154: \"Oak room\" dep Oak head room ✓.\nRow 148: \"12 seats\" dep 12 head seats ✓.\nRow 178: \"Pod\" + \"1\" separate ✓ (allowed).\nRow 189: \"the Hive\" ✓.\nRow 95: \"Felix Wong\" head Wong ✓.\nRow 85: \"Elena Popescu\" head Popescu ✓.\nRow 89: \"Marta Silva\" ✓.\nRow 92: \"Carla Mendes\" ✓.\nRow 103: \"Lee Chen\" ✓. Row 104 \"Priya Nair\" ✓. Row 106 \"Ana Ionescu\" ✓.\nRow 190: \"Pod A\" ✓. Row 181: \"Room\" + \"301\" ✓.\nRow 242: \"the budget sync\" ✓.\nRow 254: \"a call\" ✓.\nRow 255: \"a 1:1\" ✓.\nRow 258: \"a Roadmap chat\" ✓.\nRow 261: \"the launch numbers\" ✓; \"Thursday afternoon\" head afternoon dep Thursday ✓ (fine, a time nominal).\nRow 5: \"this week\" ✓.\nRow 208: \"two more seats\" ✓.\nRow 211: \"fewer seats\" ✓.\nRow 212: \"7\" ✓.\nRow 61: \"different colour\" ✓.\nRow 21: \"the house move\" ✓.\nRow 46: \"new name\" ✓.\nRow 41: \"a friendlier name\" ✓; \"Our home\" head home dep Our ✓.\nRow 198: \"a six-seater\" ✓.\nRow 133: \"any meetings\" ✓.\nRow 124: \"my people\" ✓.\nRow 125: \"the people list\" ✓.\nRow 110: \"the people list\" ✓.\nRow 31: \"the Gym calendar\" ✓.\nRow 17: \"a lime green colour\" ✓.\nRow 16: \"a Birthdays calendar\" ✓ (name as dependent, fine).\nRow 24: \"yoga retreats\" ✓.\nRow 64: \"Work\" ✓.\nRow 65: \"my calendars\" ✓.\nRow 49: \"dark blue\" head blue ✓.\nRow 52: \"a pink color\" ✓.\nRow 53: teal modifier ✓.\nRow 55: \"a bright orange\" ✓.\nRow 56: \"too much trouble\" ✓.\nRow 45: \"Studio\" ✓.\nRow 54: ✓.\n\nTime/number relations: Row 253 \"Thursday\" ✓, \"2pm\" ✓.\n\nOK, and \"at 10:30\" row 254 ✓.\n\nNow let me consider whether there are rows where a word of time/order stands after a nominal held as dependent (L3 settled case). Check:\nRow 195: \"now\" after \"the Cedar room\" but held as clause modifier ✓.\nRow 228: \"now\" held as clause modifier ✓.\nRow 105: \"now\" after \"Mike\" — held as modifier of c1 ✓ (good, not dependent).\nRow 45: \"now\" at end — modifier ✓.\nRow 76: \"now\" in c1 after \"empty\" — modifier ✓.\nRow 73: \"now\" in c2 — modifier ✓.\nRow 120: \"instead\" modifier ✓.\nRow 133: \"anymore\" modifier ✓.\nRow 2: \"back\" modifier ✓.\nRow 70: \"out\" modifier ✓.\nRow 16: \"up\" modifier ✓.\nRow 173: \"somewhere\" modifier ✓.\nRow 166: hmm \"on the top floor, above the canteen\" — fine.\n\nGood: none of these got put as a nominal dependent.\n\nWhat about \"a word of place after a nominal may be dependent or clause modifier\" — fine either way (e.g., \"somewhere quiet\").\n\nNow the last remaining question: the two rejections. Let me look at 110 once more with fresh eyes, since it's the shakiest.\n\n\"please call Ana Annie in the people list\"\nsop: c1 imperative please call complement=n1; n1 nominal dep Ana head Annie; r1 in from n1 to n2 the people list.\n\nThe construction \"call X Y\" = rename X to Y. The circuit holds \"Ana Annie\" as one nominal headed by \"Annie\". Downstream: reads \"call\" + complement \"Ana Annie\" + \"in the people list\". The next layer must undo: figure out which is the old name and which the new. The information that Ana is the person and Annie the new name — is it lost? Both words are present in order, so the next layer can see them. It's not lost, but it is a wrong statement: the nominal's head is the new name, making \"Ana\" a descriptor of \"Annie\". Under the rule \"The head is wrong\" → L3.\n\nHmm, one could argue the head is not clearly wrong: \"Ana Annie\" as a two-word name (like \"Team lunches\"). And the settled case \"A name of several words may stand whole in head, or with its last word as head and the others as dependent\". Hmm! That case is about names of several words (\"Team lunch\", \"Sales 3\"). \"call Ana Annie\" — a caller might intend to set Ana's name to \"Ana Annie\"?? No, \"call Ana Annie\" means call Ana → Annie.\n\nBut with the settled case about names of several words and about a name right after a noun... A judge might accept. Which is right? The distinguishing fact: \"call\" is a naming verb, so \"Ana\" occupies the object slot, \"Annie\" the name slot. Two complements like row 43, 120, 189. So merging them is a wrong statement about which words form each nominal; the head should be Ana (the person). I'll reject with L3.\n\nActually wait. Which is the \"wrong\" bit — head or the merge? Note: if the head were \"Ana\" with \"Annie\" dependent, per the settled case about a name after a noun... that would read \"the thing named 'Annie Ana'\"?? Hmm, no: with head Ana and dependent Annie, the nominal still merges two things, but the settled case allows \"a name right after a noun may be a dependent in that noun's nominal\" and \"Both pass\", tied to the noun. Hmm, but the settled case's dependent option is the second word as dependent. Here the second word \"Annie\" is dependent — yes in the settled reading, deps = Annie, head = Ana. Wait, the circuit has dep=Ana, head=Annie, so the FIRST word is the dependent and the second is head. In the settled reading, the first word is head and second is dependent.\n\nSo the circuit's version is precisely the disallowed one under \"Two bare nouns side by side ... either may be the head\" (that's after a marker). Here no marker. So L3: head is wrong (or two nominals merged).\n\nI'm settled: L3 for 110.\n\nNow, hold on. Let me reconsider whether \"call Ana Annie in the people list\" could be handled in a way where the merge is acceptable because both words describe the same entity's label... The next layer would think: \"call <nominal: Annie (dep Ana)>\" — perhaps reading \"Ana\" as the old label and \"Annie\" the new. Order preserved, so the next layer isn't badly harmed. But the rules list it as L3 and it is a wrong statement about the nominal's head. Reject.\n\nSimilarly, should I reconsider row 23 with equal care and think about acceptance? Statistical consistency: 2 rejections of 150. Rows 23 — coordination with an extra conjunct — that's textbook L3 \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct.\"\n\nLet me do one more careful pass over a random subset with completely fresh scrutiny, focusing on things like a modifier placed where a nominal belongs, wrong complement assignment. Let me print the rows again compactly grouped, and check each in ~1 line of reasoning. Actually I've done that above for all 150 rows. I'm reasonably confident.\n\nBut let me reconsider a couple I marked pass quickly:\n\nRow 3: \"when is my dentist appointment?\" — n1 \"my dentist appointment\". r1? none. Fine. But subject = n1 and \"when\" modifier. ✓.\n\nRow 4: \"what's on my calendar tomorrow?\" — is \"what\" the subject and relation on from c1 to n2 \"my calendar\" correct? \"what's on my calendar\" → \"what\" is subject-ish, \"on my calendar\" is a location. Yes.\n\nHmm, but the settled case says \"A question word stands where grammar puts it: ... the head of a nominal in 'what is this?'\" Here \"what's on my calendar\" parallel ✓.\n\nRow 5: \"do I have anything with Lee this week?\" ✓.\n\nRow 6: \"I'd like a separate calendar for Side project\" — \"Side project\" as a calendar name: n3 dep Side head project ✓.\n\nRow 7: \"Kids school\" dep Kids head school ✓.\n\nRow 12: \"a calendar for running\" ✓.\n\nRow 14: \"make it orange\" modifier ✓.\n\nRow 15: \"for Choir\" ✓.\n\nRow 16: \"in pink\" — n2 head \"pink\" as a nominal. \"in pink\": pink is an adjective used nominally; holding it as a nominal head is acceptable? The settled case: \"with outline gold\": \"gold\" is a dependent of \"outline\". Here \"in pink\" — no nominal before, so pink must be a nominal (as the object of \"in\"). Fine. Alternatively a modifier of the clause — not allowed since it needs a marker end. ✓ L8.\n\nRow 17: ✓.\n\nRow 19: \"give it a red colour\" ✓.\n\nRow 21: ✓.\n\nRow 30: ✓.\n\nRow 31: ✓.\n\nRow 32: ✓.\n\nRow 34: \"can you rename Family to Family and friends?\" — n2 Family complement of c1; r1 to from c1 to k1 ✓.\n\nRow 35: ✓.\n\nRow 36: ✓.\n\nRow 37: ✓.\n\nRow 41: ✓.\n\nRow 42: ✓.\n\nRow 43: ✓.\n\nRow 44: ✓.\n\nRow 45: ✓.\n\nRow 46: ✓.\n\nRow 48-56: ✓ (checked).\n\nRow 61: ✓.\n\nRow 64-82: ✓.\n\nRow 84-97: ✓ except 110.\n\nRow 102-139: ✓.\n\nRow 141-200: ✓.\n\nRow 204-272: ✓.\n\nAlright. But hold on, let me double check row 92 once more: \"I keep typing Carla's name and it doesn't find her.\" — c1 declarative keep subject I; c2 nonfinite typing complement n3 name; r3 from c1 to c2. And k1 and conjunct c1 c3 — \"and\" joins \"I keep typing...\" with \"it doesn't find her\". ✓ Correct: the \"and\" joins c1 and c3 (the clauses with finite verbs). ✓ L8.\n\nRow 56: k1 conjunct c2 c3 with no conjunction; c2 = \"I'd prefer Work in grey\", c3 = \"it's less distracting\". Semicolon separated. ✓ accept.\n\nRow 95: k1 conjunct c1 c2, no conjunction. ✓ accept.\n\nRow 105: k1 conjunct c1 c2, no conjunction (comma). ✓ accept.\n\nRow 21: k1 conjunct c3 c4, conjunction \"and\" ✓.\n\nRow 14: k1 and conjunct c1 c3 ✓ (c1 create, c3 make).\n\nRow 19: k1 and c1 c2 ✓.\n\nRow 164: ✓. Row 184: ✓. Row 204: ✓. Row 242: ✓. Row 264: ✓. Row 253: r1 with → k1 ✓. Row 259: ✓. Row 261: ✓. Row 269: ✓.\n\nHmm, row 23 is the only coordination with an extra conjunct and a real conjunction.\n\nWait — also check row 34: k1 is the end of r1, but is k1's conjunct list right? \"Family and friends\" → n3 Family, n4 friends ✓.\n\nRow 253: k1 Ana and Lee ✓.\nRow 259: k1 Lee and Mihai ✓.\nRow 261: k2 him and Priya ✓.\nRow 269: k1 Lee and Ana ✓.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, one more: does any row have a `subject` that the words don't show (L4)? e.g., imperatives with subject added? Row 21 c3/c4 no subject ✓. Row 164 c3 no subject ✓. Nonfinite clauses with subject? Row 21 c2 nonfinite \"get\" — no subject ✓. Row 220 c2 \"being\" no subject ✓. OK.\n\nDoes any row state `ellipsis`? No.\n\nSo final: 148 × L8, n=23 → L3, n=110 → L3.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider n=110 once more