m c1 to c2 exists. So per \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it\" — k1 must join c2 and c3 only. Including c1 = wrong conjunct → L3.\n\nNow write the verdicts file with 150 lines. Notes must be one sentence each. For L8, note \"\" presumably, or a short sentence. The spec says {\"n\":..., \"grade\":..., \"note\": \"<one sentence>\"}. For L8 I'll write a very short note or empty. RULES example shows note \"\" for L8. I'll use empty string for L8 to match the RULES example.\n\nLet me get the exact row order and n values to write exactly 150 lines.\n\nLet me generate the file with a script that reads n values from the CSV, and applies my verdict map (default L8, overrides for 23 and 110). That guarantees one line per row, in order.\n\nNotes:\nn=23: \"'Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar; please add it and make it gold' - 'and' joins 'add' and 'make', but the coordination also holds 'asked', a clause the message does not join with it.\"\nHmm, the note must quote words of the message in single quotes and name the wrong statement. Use single quotes only for the quotes. Avoid apostrophes inside. Let me phrase: \"'please add it and make it gold': the coordination gives 'and' three conjuncts, holding the earlier 'Mihai asked for a shared Team lunches calendar' as one of them, though no word joins it.\"\n\nn=110: \"'please call Ana Annie in the people list': 'Ana' and 'Annie' are written as one nominal headed by 'Annie', merging the person called with the name given to her, where 'Ana' is what 'call' acts on.\"\n\nGood. Now write.",
